Minor Burn Assessment and Dressing
General Practice Other
Localized thermal injury, Exquisite pain/hypersensitivity, Blister formation, TBSA <10% in adults

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-027
Reason for Visit: Acute, agonizing pain following a sudden domestic thermal injury requiring immediate analgesia and specialized wound management.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient who sustained a sudden, painful thermal burn from boiling water approximately one hour ago. The patient is experiencing severe, escalating pain and rapid blister formation, rendering them highly distressed. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to assess the total body surface area (TBSA) involved, definitively rule out a full-thickness injury requiring surgical transfer, provide immediate potent analgesia, and apply a specialized burn dressing to protect the exposed dermis. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have subjected the patient to excruciating pain and a high risk of localized wound infection overnight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I spilled a pot of boiling water down my leg, the pain is absolutely blinding, and these massive blisters are blowing up."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a recent history of a localized thermal cutaneous injury occurring roughly one hour ago. The patient reports exquisite pain and hypersensitivity to touch and air currents over the affected area. There is significant erythema, and the rapid development of intact, thin-walled fluid-filled blisters characterizing a superficial partial-thickness burn. The patient's spouse immediately applied cool running water for 20 minutes prior to calling for assistance. The pain is described as a severe, continuous burning sensation that spikes with any movement or air flow over the wound.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently immobile due to the severe pain on the right anterior thigh.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute distress; potential for significant functional impairment depending on the location and depth of the burn.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Highly distressed, diaphoretic, wincing, keeping the right leg completely still.
Physical Exam: Integumentary: Examination of the right anterior mid-thigh reveals an 8cm x 10cm area of intense erythema with three large, intact, fluid-filled blisters. There is blanching of the erythematous tissue upon applied pressure, indicating intact capillary refill and a partial-thickness depth. The total body surface area (TBSA) involvement is strictly less than 10% in an adult (estimated at 1.5%). There is a complete absence of full-thickness features such as a painless, white, or leathery eschar. The procedure requires the cleansing of the wound utilizing normal saline and the application of a non-adherent, specialized burn dressing. Expected investigation results include direct clinical visualization establishing the depth and TBSA involvement guiding outpatient management.
Environmental Observations: Kitchen shows evidence of a recent spill; environment is otherwise safe.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications; no prior analgesia taken.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Minor Burn Assessment and Dressing (Superficial Partial-Thickness Burn) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Full-Thickness (Third-Degree) Burn - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of exquisite pain (indicating intact nerve endings), brisk capillary refill (blanching), and the absence of a dry, leathery, or charred appearance.

Chemical Burn - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear, witnessed history of exposure to clean boiling water (thermal) rather than a caustic or acidic agent.

Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The immediate, instantaneous onset of the lesion corresponding exactly with the thermal trauma, rather than a gradually spreading bacterial infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient sustained an acute, highly painful superficial partial-thickness thermal burn. Telephone triage correctly identified the necessity for immediate pain control and professional wound assessment. Urgent face-to-face evaluation was mandatory to clinically map the TBSA (confirming it was safe for outpatient management), debride any ruptured blisters safely under sterile conditions, and apply a silver-impregnated or hydrogel burn dressing. The application of the specialized dressing instantly seals the exposed nerve endings from air currents, drastically reducing the severe pain. Deferring this intervention until the next in-hours period would have left the patient in agonizing pain and vulnerable to rapid bacterial colonization of the burned tissue.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Ibuprofen 400mg and Paracetamol 1g stat. Cleansed the burn gently with sterile normal saline. Intact large blisters were left unruptured to act as a biological dressing. Applied a specialized, non-adherent silver-containing hydrofiber dressing (e.g., Aquacel Ag) and secured it with a light, non-constricting crepe bandage. Administered a Tetanus toxoid booster (IM deltoid) as the patient's status was unverified.
Positive Behavioral Change: To promote optimal healing and prevent tearing the fragile new skin, commit to leaving the specialized burn dressing entirely undisturbed for the next 3 days, regardless of curiosity. Place a sticky note on the bathroom mirror that says "DO NOT TOUCH THE BANDAGE." By actively removing the visual and physical temptation to check the wound, you allow the specialized dressing to maintain the critical moist environment necessary for rapid epidermal regeneration.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Specialized burn dressings (supplied from clinical bag).
Follow-up: Mandatory GP or practice nurse review strictly in 3 days for the first dressing change and reassessment for infection.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if the pain becomes completely unmanageable despite regular painkillers, if you develop a high fever, or if foul-smelling, green pus begins leaking from under the dressing.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The severe, sharp pain is significantly reduced by the dressing. The burn will heal over 10 to 14 days. The blister may pop naturally under the dressing, which is normal.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek urgent medical attention tomorrow if you notice a rapidly spreading, bright red rash moving up your leg away from the burn site.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The dressing we applied contains silver to prevent infection and is designed to stay on for several days to protect the raw nerve endings. The blisters are your body's natural band-aid; do not pop them yourself.
Actionable Steps: Keep the dressing completely dry when showering (use a plastic bag). Take Paracetamol and Ibuprofen regularly over the next 48 hours to stay ahead of the pain. Elevate your leg whenever sitting.

